            {
              "kind": "method",
              "name": "Cause",
              "flags": [
                "exported",
                "pointer_receiver"
              ],
              "attrs": {
                "doc": "Cause returns why the attempt did not succeed.\n\nTRANSLATED, and this doc previously said the opposite. The earlier reading was that a getter's optional form would be \"reading intent from a shape, not proving it\", so the engine refused and named the missing proof. What changed is that there IS a proof: every return of this sole-failure-result function reads a field of the RECEIVER, which is the same proof the borrowed string getter one type over already makes. The claim is not that this is semantically a getter -- it is that the value handed back is a stored field that may be absent, and an optional borrow says exactly that. `Result` would say the operation succeeded or failed, which is a claim about an operation that is not here: `Ok(())` would mean \"there is no cause\".\n\nThe fence this fixture exists for is unchanged and is carried by `Check` below."
              },
              "children": [
